1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,828
Welcome to part two.

2
00:00:01,828 --> 00:00:11,992
In this video we walk through the Power BI file: how the
data is loaded, how the model and measures are built, and
what each page of the dashboard shows.

3
00:00:13,392 --> 00:00:15,291
Here is the process flow.

4
00:00:15,291 --> 00:00:18,709
We load the four CSV files from the notebook.

5
00:00:18,709 --> 00:00:22,431
Power Query sets the folder and the column types.

6
00:00:22,431 --> 00:00:26,228
The data model holds four tables with sort orders.

7
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:29,570
Twenty two DAX measures do the calculations.

8
00:00:29,570 --> 00:00:35,191
Four report pages show the results, with synced slicers for
interactivity.

9
00:00:35,191 --> 00:00:40,507
Finally the file is saved as a P B I X file and as a Power
BI project.

10
00:00:41,907 --> 00:00:46,629
Step one loads the four CSV files written by the final
notebook.

11
00:00:46,629 --> 00:00:50,761
The main table has one row for each of the 302 patients.

12
00:00:50,761 --> 00:00:58,729
The other three are small summary tables: the factor
ranking, the data quality log, and the data dictionary.

13
00:01:00,129 --> 00:01:01,839
Step two is Power Query.

14
00:01:01,839 --> 00:01:10,389
A single parameter called DataFolder holds the folder path,
so if the project moves you only edit one value and refresh.

15
00:01:10,389 --> 00:01:17,230
Each query reads its CSV file, promotes the headers, and
gives every column the right data type.

16
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:20,604
Step three is the data model.

17
00:01:20,604 --> 00:01:27,003
There are four tables, and no relationships are needed
because the summary tables stand alone.

18
00:01:27,003 --> 00:01:30,747
Ten banded columns are sorted by a hidden order column.

19
00:01:30,747 --> 00:01:37,486
Without this, Power BI would sort alphabetically, and
seventy plus would appear before under forty.

20
00:01:38,886 --> 00:01:44,033
Step four builds twenty two DAX measures, grouped into five
folders.

21
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:49,028
The core ones are total patients, disease cases, and disease
rate.

22
00:01:49,028 --> 00:01:56,370
Overall disease rate uses REMOVEFILTERS, so it stays at 46
percent and works as a reference line.

23
00:01:56,370 --> 00:02:02,047
Others cover the patient profile, the risk tiers, labels,
and data quality.

24
00:02:03,447 --> 00:02:05,919
Step five is the report.

25
00:02:05,919 --> 00:02:08,493
Page one is the overview.

26
00:02:08,493 --> 00:02:16,527
Number one: six KPI cards, with 302 patients, 138 cases and
a 46 percent rate.

27
00:02:16,527 --> 00:02:19,101
Two: the diagnosis donut.

28
00:02:19,101 --> 00:02:26,311
Three: the rate by age group, with a dashed line for the
overall rate.

29
00:02:26,311 --> 00:02:28,577
Four: the rate by sex.

30
00:02:28,577 --> 00:02:32,284
Five: a heatmap of sex by age group.

31
00:02:32,284 --> 00:02:34,447
And six: the slicers.

32
00:02:35,847 --> 00:02:38,860
Page two covers symptoms and tests.

33
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:43,938
One: chest-pain type, where no chest pain shows 73 percent.

34
00:02:43,938 --> 00:02:45,918
Two: the imaging tests.

35
00:02:45,918 --> 00:02:49,791
Three: the four stress test measures in pink.

36
00:02:49,791 --> 00:02:55,386
Four: the routine checks in grey, which barely separate
patients.

37
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:58,743
The colours tell the story at a glance.

38
00:03:00,143 --> 00:03:03,749
Page three shows risk drivers and tiers.

39
00:03:03,749 --> 00:03:05,913
One: the factor ranking.

40
00:03:05,913 --> 00:03:14,386
Two: the high-risk KPIs: 92 patients, 30 percent of
patients, holding 62 percent of all cases.

41
00:03:14,386 --> 00:03:20,877
Three and four: the disease rate by tier and by number of
warning signs.

42
00:03:20,877 --> 00:03:22,770
Five: the tier table.

43
00:03:24,170 --> 00:03:28,501
Page four is about data quality, which is business problem
one.

44
00:03:28,501 --> 00:03:39,226
It shows the counts, the issue log with every fix, the story
of the reversed label, and the data dictionary, so anyone
can trust and understand the numbers.

45
00:03:40,626 --> 00:03:42,685
Step six is interactivity.

46
00:03:42,685 --> 00:03:50,998
Four slicers, for sex, age group, chest-pain type and risk
tier, are synced across the first three pages.

47
00:03:50,998 --> 00:03:54,877
Clicking any bar cross-filters the other visuals.

48
00:03:54,877 --> 00:04:02,082
For example, select male and sixty to sixty nine, and the
disease rate jumps to 74 percent.

49
00:04:03,482 --> 00:04:05,608
Step seven saves and shares.

50
00:04:05,608 --> 00:04:10,010
The P B I X file holds the dashboard with its data inside.

51
00:04:10,010 --> 00:04:17,829
The same dashboard is also saved as a Power BI project, as
text files, so every change shows up in Git.

52
00:04:17,829 --> 00:04:23,902
To refresh on another computer, edit the DataFolder
parameter and click refresh.

53
00:04:23,902 --> 00:04:26,483
Every number matches the notebook.

54
00:04:27,883 --> 00:04:35,724
That completes the walkthrough: from raw data, through the
notebook, to an interactive Power BI dashboard.

55
00:04:35,724 --> 00:04:37,426
Thank you for watching.
